<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">UNESCO</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">UNESCO, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization, is a specialised agency of the United Nations founded in 1945 and based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. It promotes international cooperation in education, science, culture and communication. Through the World Heritage Convention of 1972 it keeps the World Heritage List of cultural and natural sites of outstanding universal value, and it also records the intangible cultural heritage of humanity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/unesco.jpg" alt="UNESCO" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mouagip · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_UNESCO.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The organisation's mandate for international cooperation can be traced to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> resolution of 21 September 1921, which asked for a commission to study whether nations could freely share cultural, educational and scientific achievements. The resulting International Committee on Intellectual Cooperation was created in 1922. Its members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-bergson">Henri Bergson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-curie">Marie Curie</a>, Robert A. Millikan and Gonzague de Reynold, and it was a small body centred largely on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</p><p>The International Institute for Intellectual Cooperation was set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in September 1924 as the committee's executing agency. The International Bureau of Education began work in December 1925 as a non-governmental body; it formed a joint commission with UNESCO in 1952 and joined it in 1969. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> largely interrupted the work of the predecessor bodies, and UNESCO was founded in 1945 as their successor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>UNESCO is governed by a General Conference of member states and associate members, which meets every two years to set its programmes and budget. The conference elects an executive board of 58 members who hold office for four years, and appoints a Director-General every four years. The headquarters stand at the Place de Fontenoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, built by a group of architects including Bernard Zehrfuss, Marcel Breuer and Luigi Nervi. The grounds hold a Garden of Peace, given by the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and designed by Isamu Noguchi in 1958.</p><p>Its work falls under five programme areas: education, natural sciences, social and human sciences, culture, and communication and information. It adopted the Convention against Discrimination in Education in 1960 and the Seville Statement on Violence in 1989, and it designates places and projects of significance, among them biosphere reserves, begun in 1971, and the Memory of the World register, begun in 1997.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the 1970s and 1980s UNESCO's support for a New World Information and Communication Order was condemned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> as an attempt to curb freedom of the press. The United States withheld its contributions and withdrew in 1984, and the United Kingdom followed in 1985. Singapore also left at the end of 1985, citing rising membership fees. The United Kingdom rejoined after a change of government in 1997, the United States in 2003 and Singapore on 8 October 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “UNESCO” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…won the Eurovision Song Contest in 2011 and the city hosted it in 2012, then the 2015 European Games, the first Formula One Grand Prix in 2016, the Islamic Solidarity Games in 2017 and UEFA Euro 2020, and <b>UNESCO</b> has recognised mugham, ashiq art and the Azerbaijani carpet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Services make 59.4 percent of GDP and agriculture 6.1; wine, made for millennia in kvevri jars that <b>UNESCO</b> listed in 2013, suffered a Russian embargo in 2006.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its records from 1884 to 1917, held by the Tanzania National Archives, were inscribed in <b>UNESCO</b>'s Memory of the World Register in 1997.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Annals of the Joseon Dynasty and the Diaries of the Royal Secretariat are among the most detailed state records of any premodern country and are inscribed on <b>UNESCO</b>'s Memory of the World register.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its dzongs are on <b>UNESCO</b>'s tentative list, the mask dance of the drums from Drametse is on the Intangible Cultural Heritage list, and in 2024 Bhutan ranked 21st on the Global Peace Index, the most peaceful country in South Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the court sanctuaries, played by drummers descended from the families who guarded them, became Burundi’s best-known art; Burundian drummers toured abroad from the 1960s, and in 2014 <b>UNESCO</b> inscribed the ritual dance of the royal drum on its Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
